{"content_hash": "bda657416cd5fdeb", "version_id": 176, "article_id": 18, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "口の中の細菌が、はいを肺炎から守るかもしれない", "title_zh": "嘴里的细菌，也许能帮肺抵抗肺炎", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-common-airway-bacterium-lungs-pneumonia.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2024/lung-1.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-07", "word_count": 267, "reading_minutes": 0.8, "lead": "口や気道には、たくさんの細菌がいます。新しい研究で、その細菌が肺を守るのを助けるかもしれないと分かりました。", "lead_zh": "口腔和气道里生活着很多细菌。一项新研究发现，这些细菌也许能帮肺抵御感染。", "preview": "アメリカのコロラド大学の研究者が、マウスで実験をしました。口と気道にいるプレボテラという細菌を、マウスに触れさせました。", "preview_zh": "美国科罗拉多大学的研究人员用小鼠做了实验。他们让小鼠接触到口腔和气道里的一种叫普雷沃氏菌的细菌。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 166, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@d7bba21/content/audio/18/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 55.938, "size_bytes": 339408, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 175, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@d7bba21/content/data/versions/176.json", "paragraphs": ["アメリカのコロラド大学の研究者が、マウスで実験をしました。口と気道にいるプレボテラという細菌を、マウスに触れさせました。", "その後、マウスに肺炎を起こす肺炎球菌を入れました。すると、マウスの免疫細胞が、この細菌をよく見つけて、食べて、消しました。", "プレボテラに触れたマウスの肺では、肺炎球菌がとても少なくなりました。免疫の細胞がもっとたくさんいて、よく働いたからです。", "研究のリーダー、サラ・クラークさんは言います。「口の細菌は、免疫を勉強させているのかもしれません」と。", "この研究は、なぜ人によって肺炎にかかりやすいか、考える手がかりになります。将来、肺炎を防ぐ新しい方法になるかもしれません。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["美国科罗拉多大学的研究人员用小鼠做了实验。他们让小鼠接触到口腔和气道里的一种叫普雷沃氏菌的细菌。", "之后，研究人员给小鼠注入了会引发肺炎的肺炎链球菌。结果，小鼠的免疫细胞能更好地发现、吞掉并清除这种细菌。", "接触过普雷沃氏菌的小鼠，肺里的肺炎链球菌少了很多。这是因为免疫细胞更多，工作得也更好。", "研究负责人莎拉·克拉克说：“嘴里的细菌，也许正在给免疫系统上课。”", "这项研究有助于解释为什么有的人更容易得肺炎。将来，它也许能带来预防肺炎的新办法。"], "body": "アメリカのコロラド大学の研究者が、マウスで実験をしました。口と気道にいるプレボテラという細菌を、マウスに触れさせました。\n\nその後、マウスに肺炎を起こす肺炎球菌を入れました。すると、マウスの免疫細胞が、この細菌をよく見つけて、食べて、消しました。\n\nプレボテラに触れたマウスの肺では、肺炎球菌がとても少なくなりました。免疫の細胞がもっとたくさんいて、よく働いたからです。\n\n研究のリーダー、サラ・クラークさんは言います。「口の細菌は、免疫を勉強させているのかもしれません」と。\n\nこの研究は、なぜ人によって肺炎にかかりやすいか、考える手がかりになります。将来、肺炎を防ぐ新しい方法になるかもしれません。", "body_zh": "美国科罗拉多大学的研究人员用小鼠做了实验。他们让小鼠接触到口腔和气道里的一种叫普雷沃氏菌的细菌。\n\n之后，研究人员给小鼠注入了会引发肺炎的肺炎链球菌。结果，小鼠的免疫细胞能更好地发现、吞掉并清除这种细菌。\n\n接触过普雷沃氏菌的小鼠，肺里的肺炎链球菌少了很多。这是因为免疫细胞更多，工作得也更好。\n\n研究负责人莎拉·克拉克说：“嘴里的细菌，也许正在给免疫系统上课。”\n\n这项研究有助于解释为什么有的人更容易得肺炎。将来，它也许能带来预防肺炎的新办法。", "text": "アメリカのコロラド大学の研究者が、マウスで実験をしました。口と気道にいるプレボテラという細菌を、マウスに触れさせました。 その後、マウスに肺炎を起こす肺炎球菌を入れました。すると、マウスの免疫細胞が、この細菌をよく見つけて、食べて、消しました。 プレボテラに触れたマウスの肺では、肺炎球菌がとても少なくなりました。免疫の細胞がもっとたくさんいて、よく働いたからです。 研究のリーダー、サラ・クラークさんは言います。「口の細菌は、免疫を勉強させているのかもしれません」と。 この研究は、なぜ人によって肺炎にかかりやすいか、考える手がかりになります。将来、肺炎を防ぐ新しい方法になるかもしれません。", "vocab": [{"word": "細菌", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细菌", "note": "さいきん。很小的生物。"}, {"word": "肺炎", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肺炎", "note": "はいえん。肺的病。"}, {"word": "肺", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肺", "note": "はい。呼吸的器官。"}, {"word": "免疫", "pos": "noun", "zh": "免疫", "note": "めんえき。身体抵抗疾病的能力。"}, {"word": "細胞", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细胞", "note": "さいぼう。身体很小的组成部分。"}, {"word": "守る", "pos": "verb", "zh": "保护，守护", "note": "まもる。"}, {"word": "研究", "pos": "noun", "zh": "研究", "note": "けんきゅう。"}, {"word": "実験", "pos": "noun", "zh": "实验", "note": "じっけん。为了验证而做的测试。"}], "title_original": "Common airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia", "summary_original": "A common bacterium found in the mouth and airways may help the lungs better defend themselves against pneumonia, according to new research from the University of Colorado Anschutz.", "body_original": "Common airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia\nby\nUniversity of Colorado Anschutz\nedited by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\npeer-reviewed publication\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nA common bacterium found in the mouth and airways may help the lungs better defend themselves against pneumonia, according to new research from the University of Colorado Anschutz.\nThe study, published in\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n, found that exposure to Prevotella melaninogenica (P. melaninogenica), a type of bacterium commonly found in healthy airways, changed the behavior of immune cells in the lungs of mice. When the mice were later exposed to Streptococcus pneumoniae, a leading cause of bacterial pneumonia, their immune systems were better able to recognize, engulf and clear the bacteria.\nThe findings offer new insight into why some people are more resilient to respiratory infections than others and could eventually point toward new ways of strengthening the lungs' natural defenses against pneumonia.\n\"Our lungs encounter bacteria from our mouths and upper airways all the time, and those exposures may actually be helping to educate the immune system,\" said Sarah Clark, Ph.D., senior author and associate professor at the University of Colorado Anschutz School of Medicine. \"We found that exposure to P. melaninogenica changes the way immune cells respond, essentially putting them in a better position to fight a bacterial infection.\"\nPreparing the immune system\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is a major cause of pneumonia, but exposure to the bacterium does not always lead to serious illness. Researchers have increasingly suspected that the community of microorganisms living throughout the airway, known as the respiratory microbiome, may play an important role in determining how well someone fights an infection.\nPrevotella is one of the most common groups of bacteria found in the healthy mouth and respiratory tract. Previous research has found that higher levels of Prevotella are associated with better outcomes in people with respiratory infections.\nBut scientists did not know exactly how the bacterium might help protect against pneumonia.\nTo investigate, Clark and her colleagues exposed mice to P. melaninogenica and then challenged them with S. pneumoniae. The researchers used\nsingle-cell RNA sequencing\nto examine thousands of individual cells in the lungs and determine how their activity changed.\nThe results showed that Prevotella exposure essentially reprogrammed several types of immune cells involved in fighting infection.\nIn mice exposed to Prevotella, immune cells called neutrophils and macrophages were better at engulfing and killing S. pneumoniae. The mice also had significantly lower amounts of the pneumonia-causing bacteria in their lungs.\n\"The immune cells were present in greater numbers and behaving differently,\" Clark said. \"They had been functionally reprogrammed to do a better job of clearing bacteria.\"\nThe lung microbiome may be part of the body's first line of defense\nThe study adds to growing evidence that bacteria normally found in the respiratory tract are not simply passive passengers. Instead, they may actively influence how the immune system responds to disease.\nThe researchers note that clinical studies have linked greater respiratory microbial diversity and the presence of anaerobic bacteria such as Prevotella with better outcomes in people with severe respiratory illness.\nThe new findings provide a possible biological explanation for those observations.\n\"We're interested in understanding whether we can harness this natural interaction between the microbiome and the immune system,\" Clark said. \"If we can figure out how these bacteria educate immune cells to respond more effectively, it could open up entirely new approaches to preventing respiratory infections.\"\nPublication details\nSara N. Stoner et al, Myeloid cell reprogramming drives enhanced defense against Streptococcus pneumoniae lung infection following exposure to commensal Prevotella,\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1073/pnas.2623525123\nJournal information:\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\nKey medical concepts\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\nPneumonia, Bacterial\nMacrophages\nNeutrophils\nClinical categories\nPulmonary medicine\nAllergy and immunology\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nInfectious diseases\nProvided by\nUniversity of Colorado Anschutz\nWho's behind this story?\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nCommon airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia (2026, October 7)\nretrieved 7 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-common-airway-bacterium-lungs-pneumonia.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:11:44.036050", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "アメリカ", "s": 0.163, "e": 0.588, "sm": 162, "em": 587, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "の", "s": 0.6, "e": 0.75, "sm": 600, "em": 750, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "コロラド", "s": 0.762, "e": 1.225, "sm": 762, "em": 1225, "cs": 5, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "大学", "s": 1.238, "e": 1.675, "sm": 1237, "em": 1675, "cs": 9, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "の", "s": 1.688, "e": 1.8, "sm": 1687, "em": 1800, "cs": 11, "ce": 12, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "研究者", "s": 1.812, "e": 2.438, "sm": 1812, "em": 2437, "cs": 12, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "が", "s": 2.45, "e": 2.675, "sm": 2450, "em": 2675, "cs": 15, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "、", "s": 2.675, "e": 2.962, "sm": 2675, "em": 2962, "cs": 16, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "マウス", "s": 2.962, "e": 3.337, "sm": 2962, "em": 3337, "cs": 17, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "で", "s": 3.35, "e": 3.462, "sm": 3350, "em": 3462, "cs": 20, "ce": 21, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "実験", "s": 3.475, "e": 3.95, "sm": 3475, "em": 3950, "cs": 21, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "を", "s": 3.9